INT. COURTHOUSE STAIRWELL - DAY

Concrete steps, a steel railing, a window of wired glass. Muffled footsteps echo from other floors.

CONSTANCE ADEYEMI, forties, a defense lawyer in a navy suit, hair pulled back so tight it pulls her eyes, leans against the railing with a coffee she hasn't touched. RONAN BLAKE, thirties, her client, in a borrowed tie two inches too short, holds a rolled-up folder like a club.

RONAN
You said it would be quick.

CONSTANCE
It is quick.

RONAN
It's been four hours.

CONSTANCE
Four hours is quick for murder.

RONAN
(small laugh)
Manslaughter.

CONSTANCE
Don't say the word out loud.

He nods. Goes quiet. Down the stairwell a door bangs; both of them turn their heads, wait, then relax.

CONSTANCE (CONT'D)
The jury is back at three.

RONAN
How do I look?

She studies him: the tie, the folder, the way his shoulders curl inward. She reaches out and unrolls the folder, flattens it, hands it back.

CONSTANCE
Carry it like a book. Not like a weapon.

He does. Straightens his tie. It doesn't help.

RONAN
Will they believe me?

CONSTANCE
They'll believe what you show them.

RONAN
I'm not good at showing.

CONSTANCE
I know.

She sets her cup on the rail. Takes out a handkerchief and pushes it into his hand.

CONSTANCE (CONT'D)
When they read the verdict, look at the jury. Not at me. Not at the floor.

RONAN
Why?

CONSTANCE
Because they remember who looked.

He folds the handkerchief into a neat square and slips it into his breast pocket, where it sits crooked and white.

RONAN
You've done this before.

CONSTANCE
Yes.

RONAN
How many times?

CONSTANCE
Enough.

She picks up the cup again, and this time she drinks.
